le":"Child Process Node.js v6.2.1-rc.1 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v6.2.1-rc.1/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v6.8.0/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v8.6.0/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process Node.js v4.0.0-rc.4 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v4.0.0-rc.4/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v8.2.1/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process Node.js v0.11.13 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/docs/v0.11.13/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v6.11.4-rc.2/docs/api/child_process.html"}]},"I found the documentation for Node.js `child_process.spawnSync`. Here are the key details:\n\n## Overview\n`child_process.spawnSync()` provides equivalent functionality to `spawn()` in a synchronous manner that blocks the event loop until the spawned process either exits or is terminated.\n\n## Key Characteristics\n- The `child_process.spawnSync()` method is synchronous and will block the Node.js event loop, pausing execution of any additional code until the spawned process exits.\n- Blocking calls like these are mostly useful for simplifying general purpose scripting tasks and for simplifying the loading/processing of application configuration at startup.\n\n## Function Signature\nThe function takes the following parameters: command (String - the command to run), args (Array - list of string arguments), and options.\n\n## Return Value\nThe function returns an Object with the following properties: pid (Process ID of the child process), output (Array of results from stdio output), stdout (Buffer or String - the contents of output[1]), stderr (Buffer or String - the contents of output[2]), and status (The exit code of the child process).\n